ASSISTANT GUIDANCE
Validation was added in small HTTP-middleware steps. Common validation checked that `mode` was exactly `blacklist` or `whitelist` and that `values` was a non-empty array.
Specific middleware then validated IPv4 octets, plain domains without protocol/path/port, and integer ports in the inclusive range 1–65535. The middleware was connected to the three POST routes from left to right before the controller.
POST /ips      → common → IPv4 → controller
POST /domains  → common → domain → controller
POST /ports    → common → port → controller
13. Validating IDs and preventing partial mutations
USER PROMPT
What is next for DELETE and PATCH?
ASSISTANT GUIDANCE
Reusable middleware required `ids` to be a non-empty array of integers. PATCH also required `active` to be a Boolean.
For missing rules, the use cases first loaded all rules and verified that every requested ID existed before deleting or updating. This corrected an earlier approach that could partially delete valid IDs before discovering a missing one.
valid format but missing rule
→ use case throws RULE_NOT_FOUND
→ controller returns HTTP 404
→ no partial delete/update
